"A program dependence graph exposes which operations depend on values and which depend on control decisions.\n\nUse when: Explain program slicing or change impact. Review whether operations can be reordered safely.\n\nAvoid when: Only the order of basic-block execution is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote statements or operations in a stated program representation. Labeled edge styles distinguish data dependence from control dependence. Optional memory-dependence labels preserve aliasing uncertainty.\n\nBuild: Choose a source region and specify the analysis abstraction. Identify value definitions and their uses. Add control dependencies and relevant memory dependencies separately. Place strongly related operations together and route each edge family through distinct corridors.\n\nCheck: Every edge names its dependence kind and direction. Control dependence is not substituted for ordinary CFG reachability. Alias uncertainty is retained rather than asserted away. Parallel dependence labels do not overlap instructions or arrowheads.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a dependence graph as a schedule with implicit timing. Claiming safe parallelism from incomplete memory analysis.\n\nExample: An amount calculation depends on a price definition, while saving the amount is controlled by a validation condition.\n\nAlternatives: Control-flow graph, Call graph."
